{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cryptographic Hash Function.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cryptographic Hash Function.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cryptographic hash function cryptographic hash function","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cryptographic Hash Function.md","fileName":"Cryptographic Hash Function.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cryptographic Hash Function","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-08 21:16\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[.NET Cryptography and Access Control]] [[Applied Cryptography and PKI]]","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Cryptography and Access Control","Applied Cryptography and PKI","c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf","cybersecurity.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cryptographic Hash Function.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cryptographic Hash Function.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cryptographic hash function cryptographic hash function","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cryptographic Hash Function.md","fileName":"Cryptographic Hash Function.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cryptographic Hash Function","heading":"Cryptographic Hash Function","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A cryptographic hash function maps data of arbitrary length to a fixed-size digest. The operation is designed to be one-way, and a useful secure hash makes it impractical to find a different input with the same digest.\n\nHashes can reveal whether data changed, but an unkeyed digest alone cannot establish who produced it because anyone can recompute the value. Password storage, message authentication, and digital signatures therefore build on hashing with additional mechanisms suited to each threat model.\n\nIntegrity checking compares a newly computed digest with a trusted earlier digest: unchanged data should produce the same result. The source treats MD5 and SHA-1 as compromised choices and presents the SHA-2 family as their stronger successor for integrity applications.","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Cryptography and Access Control","Applied Cryptography and PKI","c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf","cybersecurity.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cryptographic Hash Function.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cryptographic Hash Function.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cryptographic hash function cryptographic hash function","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cryptographic Hash Function.md","fileName":"Cryptographic Hash Function.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cryptographic Hash Function","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf]]\n\n[[cybersecurity.epub]]","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Cryptography and Access Control","Applied Cryptography and PKI","c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf","cybersecurity.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}